Communication between Unix computers

The cu program is used to communicate between Unix computers.
There is some setup required:

The port to be used must NOT be a login port -- that is why we have
not enabled /dev/tty0 (serial a) as a login port. Use "serial a" for cu.

The speed of transmission MUST be entered into the file /usr/lib/uucp/L-devices.
As we supply the system, that file has:

		DIR	tty0	tty0	9600

This means that cu can occur only on /dev/tty0 (serial a) at a speed
of 9600. To change the speed, just put in the correct speed. You may
have more than one line in this file, as in:

		DIR	tty0	tty0	9600
		DIR	tty1	tty1	1200

In the above example, /dev/tty0 runs at 9600,
and /dev/tty1 runs at 1200 baud. Remember that cu
will only run on a non-login port!

To run cu, enter:

cu -s1200 -l /dev/tty1 dir

	Note that the -s flag which controls the speed requires the
	speed indicator to be right next to the "-s", as seen
	above.

	The "-l" flag controls the port to be used. (You must have
	a space between the "-l" and the port.)

You should then get a "Connected" message. If you get an error message,
first look at /usr/spool/uucp/LCK*. If any such files exist, remove them.
(This would mean that a cu session terminated abnormally.)

You MAY need a modem eliminator (depending upon your cables.)

To initiate communication after the "Connected" message, type
whatever is needed to run a command on the remote machine, as in
ATDT8005551212 to start a Hayes modem.

To copy a file from the remote computer onto the Lisa, use the
~%take command after cu is running. To copy a file from the Lisa
onto the remote computer, run the ~%put command.

CU IS VERY POWERFUL AND COMPLEX: PLEASE READ THE MANUAL PAGES IN SECTION 1.
